-digit number with digit sum 27 is 999. Similarly, for other values, perhaps R can be different. But if R is not all 9s, maybe the congruence can be adjusted.\n\nWait, the key is that R can be any number with digit sum s and satisfying R \u2261 - (M \u00d710^k) mod223. So, if we can find such an R, then we can have the entire number divisible by 223. The question is whether such an R exists.\n\nLet's formalize this. Let M be a number consisting of n 9s, so M = (10^n -1)/9. Then, N = M \u00d710^k + R. We need N \u22610 mod223. So:\n\n(M \u00d710^k + R) \u22610 mod223\n=> R \u2261 -M \u00d710^k mod223\n\nWe need R to be a k-digit number with digit sum s=2007 -9n. Let's fix k, say k=3. Then, R is a 3-digit number (from 000 to 999), and s=27 (if n=220). So we need R \u2261 -M \u00d710^3 mod223, and R has digit sum 27.\n\nBut M = (10^n -1)/9. When n=220, M = (10^220 -1)/9. Therefore, M mod223 can be computed. Let's compute M mod223.\n\nSince 10^222 \u22611 mod223 (by Fermat's little theorem), so 10^220 =10^(222 -2) \u226110^(-2) mod223. Compute 10^(-2) mod223. First, find the inverse of 10 modulo223. Since 223\u00d722=4906, 223\u00d722 + 10=4916, so 10 \u2261 -223\u00d722 mod223. Wait, perhaps better to use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFind x such that 10x \u22611 mod223.\n\n223 = 10\u00d722 + 3\n\n10 = 3\u00d73 + 1\n\n3 =1\u00d73 +0\n\nSo gcd is 1. Backwards:\n\n1 =10 -3\u00d73\n\nBut 3=223 -10\u00d722, so:\n\n1=10 - (223 -10\u00d722)\u00d73\n\n=10 -223\u00d73 +10\u00d766\n\n=10\u00d767 -223\u00d73\n\nTherefore, 10\u00d767 \u22611 mod223. So the inverse of 10 mod223 is 67. Therefore, 10^(-1) \u226167 mod223. Then, 10^(-2) \u226167^2 mod223. Compute 67^2: 67\u00d767=4489. Divide 4489 by223:\n\n223\u00d720=4460, 4489-4460=29. So 67^2 \u226129 mod223.\n\nTherefore, 10^220 \u226129 mod223.\n\nTherefore, M=(10^220 -1)/9 \u2261 (29 -1)/9 mod223. Since 29 -1=28, so 28/9 mod223.\n\nBut division in modular arithmetic requires multiplying by the inverse. 9^{-1} mod223. Let's compute it.\n\nUsing the extended Euclidean algorithm again:\n\n223=9\u00d724 +7\n\n9=7\u00d71 +2\n\n7=2\u00d73 +1\n\n2=1\u00d72 +0\n\nBackwards:\n\n1=7 -2\u00d73\n\nBut 2=9 -7\u00d71, so:\n\n1=7 - (9 -7\u00d71)\u00d73 =7\u00d74 -9\u00d73\n\nBut 7=223 -9\u00d724, so:\n\n1=(223 -9\u00d724)\u00d74 -9\u00d73 =223\u00d74 -9\u00d796 -9\u00d73 =223\u00d74 -9\u00d799\n\nTherefore, 1 \u2261 -9\u00d799 mod223, so -9\u00d799 \u22611 mod223 \u2192 9\u00d7(-99)\u22611 mod223 \u2192 9\u00d7124\u22611 mod223 (since -99 mod223 is 124). Therefore, 9^{-1} \u2261124 mod223.\n\nSo back to M=(10^220 -1)/9 \u2261 (29 -1)/9 \u226128/9 \u226128\u00d7124 mod223.\n\nCompute 28\u00d7124:\n\n28\u00d7100=2800\n\n28\u00d724=672\n\nTotal=2800+672=3472\n\nNow divide 3472 by223:\n\n223\u00d715=3345\n\n3472-3345=127\n\nSo 3472\u2261127 mod223. Therefore, M \u2261127 mod223.\n\nThen, M \u00d710^3 mod223. Since M\u2261127, 10^3=1000. 1000 mod223: 223\u00d74=892, 1000-892=108. So 1000\u2261108 mod223.\n\nTherefore, M\u00d710^3 \u2261127\u00d7108 mod223.\n\nCompute 127\u00d7108:\n\n127\u00d7100=12700\n\n127\u00d78=1016\n\nTotal=12700+1016=13716\n\nNow divide 13716 by223:\n\n223\u00d761=223\u00d760 +223=13380 +223=13603\n\n13716 -13603=113\n\nSo 127\u00d7108\u2261113 mod223.\n\nTherefore, R\u2261-113 mod223. Since R is a 3-digit number, R=223 -113=110 (but wait, -113 mod223 is 223-113=110). However, R must be a 3-digit number, so 110 is 3-digit (as 110), but its digit sum is 1+1+0=2, which is not 27. However, we needed R to have a digit sum of 27. But 110's digit sum is 2, which is way less. Therefore, this approach doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps R can be 110 +223\u00d7t, where t is an integer, such that R is still a 3-digit number and has digit sum 27. Let's see:\n\nR=110 +223t. Since R must be a 3-digit number (100\u2264R\u2264999), let's find t such that 100 \u2264110 +223t \u2264999.\n\nSolve for t:\n\nLower bound: 110 +223t \u2265100 \u2192 always true since t\u22650.\n\nUpper bound:11