f the base is \\( \\frac{c}{2} \\). The height \\( h \\) of the pyramid is equal to this distance because the angle of inclination is \\( 45^\\circ \\):\n\n\\[\nh = \\frac{c}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe volume of the pyramid is then calculated using the formula for the volume of a pyramid (\\( \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\text{base area} \\times \\text{height} \\)):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Volume} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{c^2 \\sqrt{3}}{8} \\times \\frac{c}{2} = \\frac{c^3 \\sqrt{3}}{48}\n\\]\n\nThus, the volume of the pyramid is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{c^3 \\sqrt{3}}{48}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Establish triangle and tetrahedron properties:**\n\n   Consider a triangular pyramid (tetrahedron) with a right-angled triangle base $ABC$, where $\\angle C = 90^\\circ$, hypotenuse $AB = c$, and $\\angle A = 30^\\circ$. Let $DH$ be the height of the tetrahedron from vertex $D$ to the plane of triangle $ABC$. By definition, $DH$ is perpendicular to the plane $ABC$.\n\n2. **Projection of sides on the base:**\n\n   By the problem's conditions, the edges $AD$, $BD$, and $CD$ are inclined at $45^\\circ$ to the base. Hence, the projections of these edges on the base are given by angles $\\angle DAH = \\angle DBH = \\angle DCH = 45^\\circ$.\n\n3. **Properties of right-angled projections:**\n\n   Triangles $DAH$, $DBH$, and $DCH$ are right-angled triangles with one acute angle of $45^\\circ$. Therefore, all these triangles are congruent because they share one leg and the angle.\n\n4. **Confirm $H$ as the orthocenter:**\n\n   From the congruency and symmetry:\n   \\[\n   AH = BH = CH\n   \\]\n   This implies that $H$ is the center of the circumscribed circle around the base triangle $ABC$. For a right-angled triangle, this point $H$ lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse $AB$.\n\n5. **Compute height $DH$:**\n\n   Given $\\tan(45^\\circ) = 1$, the height $DH$ can be calculated as:\n   \\[\n   DH = AH \\cdot \\tan(45^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2} c \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{2} c\n   \\]\n\n6. **Sides 