.\n\nWith a + b + c=8 and ab + bc + ca=16.\n\nLet\u2019s let\u2019s fix m and see if we can find variables a, b, c.\n\nBut maybe this is getting too abstract. Let's think of variables a and c such that their product is m, and then express b from the sum.\n\nGiven a + b + c =8 => b=8 -a -c.\n\nThen ab + bc + ca=16.\n\nSubstitute b:\n\na*(8 -a -c) + (8 -a -c)*c +a*c=16\n\nExpand:\n\n8a -a\u00b2 -ac +8c -ac -c\u00b2 +ac=16\n\nSimplify terms:\n\n8a +8c -a\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -ac=16\n\nRearranged:\n\n-a\u00b2 -c\u00b2 -ac +8a +8c =16\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\na\u00b2 +c\u00b2 +ac -8a -8c = -16\n\nThis seems a bit complicated. Maybe complete the squares or something.\n\nAlternatively, set x = a, y = c. Then equation becomes x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy -8x -8y = -16.\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in two variables. Maybe we can find the maximum m=xy such that the equation holds and also ab \u2265m, bc\u2265m.\n\nBut since b=8 -x -y, ab=x*(8 -x -y) \u2265m=xy, and bc=(8 -x -y)*y \u2265xy.\n\nSo we have:\n\nx(8 -x -y) \u2265xy =>8x -x\u00b2 -xy \u2265xy =>8x -x\u00b2 \u22652xy.\n\nSimilarly, y(8 -x -y) \u2265xy =>8y -y\u00b2 -xy \u2265xy =>8y -y\u00b2 \u22652xy.\n\nSo we have two inequalities:\n\n8x -x\u00b2 \u22652xy\n\n8y -y\u00b2 \u22652xy\n\nAnd the equation:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy -8x -8y = -16\n\nThis is quite complex. Let me see if we can parameterize variables.\n\nSuppose m=xy. Let\u2019s denote m=xy. Then we need to maximize m such that:\n\n1. 8x -x\u00b2 \u22652xy=2m\n\n2. 8y -y\u00b2 \u22652xy=2m\n\n3. x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy -8x -8y = -16\n\nWe can express equations 1 and 2 as:\n\n8x -x\u00b2 \u22652m =>x(8 -x) \u22652m\n\n8y -y\u00b2 \u22652m =>y(8 -y) \u22652m\n\nSo, from these inequalities, since m=xy, we have:\n\nx(8 -x) \u22652xy =>8 -x \u22652y\n\nSimilarly, y(8 -y) \u22652xy =>8 -y \u22652x\n\nTherefore:\n\nFrom first inequality: 8 -x \u22652y\n\nFrom second inequality:8 -y \u22652x\n\nSo:\n\nFrom 1: 2y \u22648 -x => y \u2264(8 -x)/2\n\nFrom 2: 2x \u22648 -y => x \u2264(8 -y)/2\n\nBut since y \u2264(8 -x)/2, substitute into x \u2264(8 -y)/2:\n\nx \u2264(8 -y)/2 \u2264(8 - (8 -x)/2)/2= (8 -4 +x/2)/2=(4 +x/2)/2=2 +x/4\n\nThus, x \u22642 +x/4 => (3/4)x \u22642 =>x \u22648/3\u22482.666\n\nSimilarly, since x \u22648/3, and similarly y \u22648/3.\n\nSo x and y are bounded above by 8/3.\n\nAlso, since m=xy, to maximize m, we need x and y as large as possible, but constrained by x,y \u22648/3.\n\nBut also, from equation 3:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy -8x -8y = -16\n\nLet\u2019s rearrange:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy =8x +8y -16\n\nBut this is still complicated. Let\u2019s try to parametrize.\n\nLet\u2019s set x = y. Then, due to symmetry, perhaps x = y. Let\u2019s see.\n\nIf x = y, then equation becomes:\n\nx\u00b2 +x\u00b2 +x\u00b2 -8x -8x= -16 =>3x\u00b2 -16x= -16 =>3x\u00b2 -16x +16=0\n\nWhich is the same quadratic as before. Solutions:\n\nx=(16 \u00b1\u221a(256 -192))/6=(16\u00b18)/6=24/6=4 or 8/6=4/3\u22481.333.\n\nIf x=4, then y=4. Then b=8 -4 -4=0. Then ab=4*0=0, bc=0*4=0, ca=4*4=16. So m=0. Not helpful.\n\nIf x=4/3, then y=4/3. Then b=8 -4/3 -4/3=8 -8/3=16/3\u22485.333. Then ab=(4/3)*(16/3)=64/9\u22487.111, bc= same, ca=(4/3)^2=16/9\u22481.777. So m=16/9. Same as before.\n\nBut we need to check if this is indeed the maximum. Let's see if when x and y are not equal, can we get a higher m.\n\nSuppose x=8/3, then from x \u22648/3, so x=8/3\u22482.666. Then from inequality 2y \u22648 -x=8 -8/3=16/3\u22485.333 => y \u22648/3\u22482.666. So maximum y=8/3. Then m=xy=(8/3)^2=64/9\u22487.111. But substitute into equation 3:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy -8x -8y = (64/9) + (64/9) + (64/9) -8*(8/3) -8*(8/3)\n\n= 192/9 - 64/3 -64/3\n\n=64/3 - 128/3\n\n= -64/3 \u2248-21.333, which is not equal to -16. So equation not satisfied. So invalid.\n\nAlternatively, pick x=2. Then from inequality y \u2264(8 -2)/2=3. Let\u2019s choose y=3. Then m=xy=6. Check equation 3:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 +xy -8x -8y =4 +9 +6 -16 -24=19 -40=-21\u2260-16. Not valid.\n\nAlternatively, x=2.5, y\u2264(8 -2.5)/2=2.75. Let\u2019s pick y=2.5. Then m=6.25. Check equation:\n\n2.5\u00b2 +2.5\u00b2 +2.5*2.5 -8*2.5 -8*2.5=6.25+6.25+6.25 -20 -20=18.75 -40=-21.25\u2260-16. Not valid.\n\nAlternatively, take x=3, but x\u22648/3\u22482.666, so x=3 is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, x=2, y=2.4. Then m=4.8. Check equation:\n\n4 +5.76 +4.8 -16 -19.2=14.56 -35.2=-20.64\u2260-16. Still not matching.\n\nAlternatively, suppose x= (8/3) - t, y=(8/3) - s, trying near 8/3. But this might not be helpful.\n\nAlternatively, use Lagrange multipliers. Let\u2019s set up the optimization problem.\n\nWe need to maximize m, subject to:\n\na + b + c =8,\n\nab + bc + ca=16,\n\nab \u2265m,\n\nbc \u2265m,\n\nca \u2265m,\n\nand a,b,c \u22650.\n\nThis is a constrained optimization problem. The maximum m will occur at a point where all constraints are satisfied, and at least one of the products ab, bc, ca is equal to m (otherwise, we could increase m slightly).\n\nTherefore, the maximum m is the largest value such that there exist non-negative a,b,c with a + b + c =8, ab + bc + ca=16, and ab, bc, ca \u2265m.\n\nTo use Lagrange multipliers, let's consider the constraints. But since we have inequalities, it's more complex. Alternatively, consider that at the maximum m, two of the products will be equal to m, and the third will be greater or equal. For example, suppose ab = m and bc = m, then the third product ca \u2265m. Or maybe only one product equals m and the others are greater.\n\nBut given the symmetry, the maximum m likely occurs when two products are equal to m, and the third is equal or higher. Let\u2019s assume ab = bc =m, then as before, leads to a= c and solving gives m=16/9.\n\nAlternatively, suppose ab = m and ca = m, then a(b + c)=2m, but also a + b +c=8, and ab + bc + ca=16. This might not necessarily lead to a better result.\n\nAlternatively, consider that the minimal product is ab. So ab =m. We need to maximize m such that bc \u2265m and ca \u2265m.\n\nExpress