.\n\nThus, solutions for t are:\n\nt=[8(k +1) \u00b1\u221a(64k)]/[2(k\u00b2 +k +1)]\n\n= [8(k +1) \u00b18\u221ak]/[2(k\u00b2 +k +1)]\n\n=4(k +1 \u00b1\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nSince t =b >0, we take the positive root.\n\nThus, b=4(k +1 +\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1) or b=4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nBut since a =k*b and a \u2264b, k \u22641, we need to check which solution gives a \u2264b.\n\nCompute both roots:\n\nFirst root:\n\nb1=4(k +1 +\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nSecond root:\n\nb2=4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nCompare a1 =k*b1 and a2 =k*b2 with b1 and b2.\n\nFor the first root, a1 =4k(k +1 +\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1). Since k \u22641, this might be less than b1.\n\nSimilarly for a2.\n\nBut need to check which root is valid.\n\nAlternatively, let's take the second root, which subtracts \u221ak. Let's test for k=1.\n\nAt k=1, the equation becomes:\n\nb=4(1 +1 -1)/(1 +1 +1)=4(1)/3=4/3. Then a=1*4/3=4/3. So c=8 -4/3 -4/3=16/3. This is the case we had before, where a=b=4/3, c=16/3, m=ab=16/9.\n\nSimilarly, for k=1, first root gives b=4(1 +1 +1)/(1 +1 +1)=4*3/3=4. Then a=1*4=4, c=8 -4 -4=0. This gives m=ab=16, but bc=0, which is lower. So the second root is the valid one when k=1.\n\nTherefore, likely the valid solution is the second root: b=4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\na=4k(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nb=4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nNow, we need to ensure that the inequalities 8b -b\u00b2 \u22652ab and 8a -a\u00b2 \u22652ab hold.\n\nLet\u2019s compute 8b -b\u00b2 -2ab \u22650.\n\nSubstitute a= k*b:\n\n8b -b\u00b2 -2k*b\u00b2 \u22650\n\nFactor b:\n\nb(8 -b -2k*b) \u22650\n\nSince b>0, need 8 -b -2k*b \u22650 =>8 \u2265b(1 +2k)\n\nSimilarly, 8a -a\u00b2 -2ab \u22650:\n\n8a -a\u00b2 -2k*a*b \u22650\n\nFactor a:\n\na(8 -a -2k*b) \u22650\n\nSince a>0, need 8 -a -2k*b \u22650 =>8 \u2265a +2k*b\n\nBut a= k*b, so:\n\n8 \u2265k*b +2k*b=3k*b =>8 \u22653k*b\n\nBut from the previous inequality, 8 \u2265b(1 +2k). So we have two conditions:\n\n8 \u2265b(1 +2k)\n\n8 \u22653k*b\n\nSince b is expressed in terms of k, we can substitute.\n\nFrom earlier, b=4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nSo, condition1: 8 \u22654(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)*(1 +2k)\n\n=> 2 \u2265(k +1 -\u221ak)(1 +2k)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nSimilarly, condition2:8 \u22653k*4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\n=> 2 \u22653k(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)\n\nThese inequalities seem complicated, but maybe they hold for k \u2208 (0,1].\n\nAlternatively, since we are maximizing m=ab= k*b\u00b2= k*[4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)]\u00b2\n\nThis expression is quite complex. To find its maximum, we might need calculus.\n\nLet\u2019s denote m(k)=k*[4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)]\u00b2\n\n=16k(k +1 -\u221ak)^2/(k\u00b2 +k +1)^2\n\nTo find the maximum of m(k) over k \u2208(0,1].\n\nTake derivative and set to zero. This would be quite involved, but perhaps we can substitute t=\u221ak, where t \u2208(0,1].\n\nLet k=t\u00b2. Then m(t)=16t\u00b2(t\u00b2 +1 -t)^2/(t\u2074 +t\u00b2 +1)^2\n\nThis might not help much, but let's try:\n\nLet\u2019s compute derivative dm/dt and set to zero.\n\nBut this is very complicated. Alternatively, note that when k=1, m=16/9\u22481.777. Let\u2019s check at k=1/4.\n\nLet\u2019s try k=1/4. Then t=1/2.\n\nCompute b=4(k +1 -\u221ak)/(k\u00b2 +k +1)=4*(1/4 +1 -1/2)/(1/16 +1/4 +1)=4*(3/4 -1/2)/(21/16)=4*(1/4)/(21/16)=4*(4/21)=16/21\u22480.7619\n\na= k*b=1/4*16/21=4/21\u22480.1905\n\nc=8 -a -b=8 -4/21 -16/21=8 -20/21=148/21\u22487.0476\n\nCheck ab=4/21*16/21=64/441\u22480.145, bc=16/21*148/21\u224816*148/441\u22482368/441\u22485.37, ca=4/21*148/21\u2248592/441\u22481.343. So m=ab\u22480.145 <16/9. So worse.\n\nTry k=1/2.\n\nk=1/2. Compute b=4*(1/2 +1 -\u221a(1/2))/(1/4 +1/2 +1)=4*(3/2 -\u221a0.5)/(7/4)=4*(3/2 -\u221a2/2)/(7/4)=4*( (3 -\u221a2)/2 )/(7/4)=4*(4)(3 -\u221a2)/2*7= (16)(3 -\u221a2)/14\u2248(16*(1.5858))/14\u224825.3728/14\u22481.812\n\na=k*b=0.5*1.812\u22480.906\n\nc=8 -0.906 -1.812\u22485.282\n\nab\u