s an integer, \u221an must be rational. But n is an integer, so \u221an is either integer or irrational. Hence, \u221an must be an integer because otherwise it's irrational, and (k\u00b2 - p)/(2k) would have to be irrational, but the left side is \u221an which would have to be irrational. Wait, but if n is not a perfect square, then \u221an is irrational, but (k\u00b2 - p)/(2k) would have to equal that irrational number, which is possible if k\u00b2 - p is a multiple of 2k times that irrational number. But since k, p are integers, this seems only possible if \u221an is rational, i.e., n is a perfect square. Therefore, n must be a perfect square.\n\nTherefore, n must be a perfect square, so let's denote n = m\u00b2, where m is a non-negative integer. Then, p + n = p + m\u00b2. Let's denote p + m\u00b2 = l\u00b2, where l is an integer, since \u221a(p + n) must also be integer. Then, we have:\n\nl\u00b2 - m\u00b2 = p\n\nWhich factors as (l - m)(l + m) = p\n\nSince p is a prime number, its only positive divisors are 1 and p. Therefore, the factors (l - m) and (l + m) must be 1 and p. Since l and m are integers, and l > m (because l\u00b2 = p + m\u00b2 > m\u00b2), so:\n\nl - m = 1\n\nl + m = p\n\nSolving these two equations:\n\nAdding both equations:\n\n2l = p + 1 => l = (p + 1)/2\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second:\n\n2m = p - 1 => m = (p - 1)/2\n\nSince l and m must be integers, (p + 1)/2 and (p - 1)/2 must be integers. Therefore, p must be an odd prime, because if p is odd, then p - 1 and p + 1 are even, so their halves are integers. If p is 2, the only even prime, then:\n\nIf p = 2, then m = (2 - 1)/2 = 0.5, which is not an integer. Therefore, p cannot be 2. Therefore, p must be an odd prime. So all odd primes can be expressed in this way?\n\nWait, but wait. So for any odd prime p, we can set m = (p - 1)/2 and l = (p + 1)/2, which are integers because p is odd. Then, n = m\u00b2 = [(p - 1)/2]^2.\n\nTherefore, for any odd prime p, there exists an intege