ght side is (x\u2076 + 0)/2 = x\u2076 / 2. So x\u2076 /8 \u2264 x\u2076 /2. Since x\u2076 is non-negative (because any real number to the sixth power is non-negative), this inequality holds. So that works. \n\nAnother test case: let x = 1 and y = -1. Let's see. Left side: (1 + (-1))/2 = 0. So the entire left side becomes 0, and the right side is (1 + 1)/2 = 1. So 0 \u2264 1, which is true. That works too. \n\nOkay, so the test cases seem to hold. Now, how to approach the general case. Since x and y are arbitrary real numbers, but since the inequality involves even powers, maybe considering symmetry or substituting variables.\n\nAlternatively, since the inequality is symmetric in x and y, perhaps we can assume without loss of generality that x \u2265 y or something. Hmm. But not sure yet.\n\nAnother thought: Maybe use the AM \u2265 GM inequality, but here we have products and sums. Wait, the left-hand side is a product of three arithmetic means, and the right-hand side is another arithmetic mean. Maybe there's a way to apply H\u00f6lder's inequality? H\u00f6lder's inequality relates products and sums in a certain way.\n\nLet me recall H\u00f6lder's inequality: For sequences of non-negative real numbers and exponents p, q, r such that 1/p + 1/q + 1/r = 1, then the product of sums is less than or equal to the product of the L^p norms. Wait, actually, H\u00f6lder's inequality in its general form states that for conjugate exponents p, q, etc., the sum of products is less than or equal to the product of the L^p norms. But maybe there's a version that applies here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the AM-GM inequality on each term. Let me see. The left-hand side is the product of three arithmetic means. The arithmetic mean is always greater than or equal to the geometric mean. So, for each term:\n\n(x + y)/2 \u2265 \u221a(xy),\n\n(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2)/2 \u2265 \u221a(x\u00b2 y\u00b2) = |xy|,\n\n(x\u00b3 + y\u00b3)/2 \u2265 \u221a(x\u00b3 y\u00b3) = |xy|^{3/2}.\n\nBut since we are