n\nx^5 y, x y^5,\n\nx^4 y\u00b2, x\u00b2 y^4.\n\nSo altogether:\n\nLeft-hand side after expansion = x\u2076 + y\u2076 + 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 + x^5 y + x y^5 + x^4 y\u00b2 + x\u00b2 y^4.\n\nNow, the right-hand side of the inequality we need to show is 4(x\u2076 + y\u2076). So we need to prove:\n\nx\u2076 + y\u2076 + 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 + x^5 y + x y^5 + x^4 y\u00b2 + x\u00b2 y^4 \u2264 4x\u2076 + 4y\u2076\n\nSubtracting left-hand side from right-hand side, the inequality becomes:\n\n4x\u2076 + 4y\u2076 - (x\u2076 + y\u2076 + 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 + x^5 y + x y^5 + x^4 y\u00b2 + x\u00b2 y^4) \u2265 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n3x\u2076 + 3y\u2076 - 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 - x^5 y - x y^5 - x^4 y\u00b2 - x\u00b2 y^4 \u2265 0\n\nSo we need to show that 3x\u2076 + 3y\u2076 - 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 - x^5 y - x y^5 - x^4 y\u00b2 - x\u00b2 y^4 is non-negative.\n\nHmm. Maybe factor this expression. Let me denote this expression as S:\n\nS = 3x\u2076 + 3y\u2076 - 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 - x^5 y - x y^5 - x^4 y\u00b2 - x\u00b2 y^4\n\nI need to show that S \u2265 0 for all real x, y.\n\nAlternatively, factor terms. Let's see if we can group terms:\n\nNotice that S can be written as:\n\n3x\u2076 - x^5 y - x^4 y\u00b2 - 2x\u00b3 y\u00b3 + 3y\u2076 - x\u00b2 y^4 - x y^5\n\nBut not sure yet. Maybe factor out x\u00b3 from the first four terms:\n\nx\u00b3(3x\u00b3 - x\u00b2 y - x y\u00b2 - 2y\u00b3) + y\u00b3(3y\u00b3 - x\u00b2 y - x y\u00b2 - 2x\u00b3)\n\nWait, that might not be helpful. Alternatively, maybe symmetrize the expression.\n\nNote that S is symmetric in x and y. So perhaps substituting variables. Let me set t = x/y or y/x if one of them is non-zero. But if both x and y are zero, S is zero. If one is zero, say y = 0, then S = 3x\u2076 \u2265 0, which holds. So assume y \u2260 0, set t = x/y, then x = t y. Substitute into S.\n\nLet me try that. Let y \u2260 0, x = t y. Then:\n\nS = 3(t y)^6 + 3 y^6 - 2(t y)^3 y^3 - (t y)^5 y - (t y) y^5 - (t y)^4 y\u00b2 - (t y)^2 y^4\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n3 t^6 y^6 + 3 y^6 - 2 t^3 y^6 - t^5 y^6 - t y^6 - t^4 y^6 - t^2 y^6\n\nFactor out y^6:\n\ny^6 [3 t^6 + 3 - 2 t^3 - t^5 - t - t^4 - t^2]\n\nSo we have S = y^6 [3 t^6 + 3 - 2 t^3 - t^5 - t - t^4 - t^2]\n\nSince y^6 is always non-negative, we need to show that the polynomial in t is non-negative for all real t.\n\nLet me denote P(t) = 3 t^6 + 3 - 2 t^3 - t^5 - t - t^4 - t^2\n\nNeed to show P(t) \u2265 0 for all real t.\n\nHmm. Maybe factor this polynomial. Let's try to write P(t) as a sum of squares or something.\n\nAlternatively, check for specific values of t:\n\nWhen t = 1: P(1) = 3 + 3 - 2 -1 -1 -1 -1 = 6 - 6 = 0. So t=1 is a root.\n\nSimilarly, t = -1: Let's compute:\n\n3*(-1)^6 + 3 - 2*(-1)^3 - (-1)^5 - (-1) - (-1)^4 - (-1)^2\n\n= 3*1 + 3 - 2*(-1) - (-1) - (-1) - 1 -1\n\n= 3 + 3 + 2 +1 +1 -1 -1\n\n= 6 + 4 -2 = 8. So P(-1) = 8 \u22650.\n\nt = 0: P(0) = 0 + 3 -0 -0 -0 -0 -0 =3 \u22650.\n\nt=2: Compute P(2):\n\n3*64 +3 -2*8 -32 -2 -16 -4\n\n=192 +3 -16 -32 -2 -16 -4\n\n=195 -70=125 \u22650.\n\nt= -2:\n\n3*64 +3 -2*(-8) -(-32) -(-2) -16 -4\n\n=192 +3 +16 +32 +2 -16 -4\n\n=192+3=195; 195+16=211; 211+32=243; 243+2=245; 245-16=229; 229-4=225 \u22650.\n\nHmm, seems positive. What about t = 1/2:\n\nP(0.5) = 3*(1/64) +3 -2*(1/8) - (1/32) -0.5 - (1/16) - (1/4)\n\n= 3/64 + 3 - 0.25 - 0.03125 -0.5 -0.0625 -0.25\n\nConvert all to 64 denominators:\n\n3/64 + 192/64 - 16/64 - 2/64 -32/64 -4/64 -16/64\n\n= [3 +192 -16 -2 -32 -4 -16]/64\n\n= [195 -70]/64 = 125/64 \u22481.953 \u22650.\n\nSo far, all test values give P(t) \u22650. How about t approaching infinity? The leading term is 3t^6, which dominates, so P(t) tends to infinity as t\u2192\u00b1\u221e. So the polynomial is positive at extremes, zero at t=1. What about between t=0 and t=1? Let's take t=0.5 as before, which was positive. What about t approaching 1? Let me take t=0.9:\n\nCompute P(0.9):\n\n3*(0.9)^6 +3 -2*(0.9)^3 - (0.9)^5 -0.9 - (0.9)^4 - (0.9)^2\n\nFirst compute each term:\n\n0.9^2 =0.81\n\n0.9^3=0.729\n\n0.9^4=0.6561\n\n0.9^5=0.59049\n\n0.9^6=0.531441\n\nSo,\n\n3*0.531441 \u22481.594323\n\nPlus 3: \u22484.594323\n\nMinus 2*0.729 \u22481.458: \u22484.594323 -1.458\u22483.136\n\nMinus 0.59049: \u22483.136 -0.59049\u22482.54551\n\nMinus 0.9: \u22482.54551 -0.9\u22481.64551\n\nMinus 0.6561:\u22481.64551 -0.6561\u22480.98941\n\nMinus 0.81:\u22480.98941 -0.81\u22480.17941 \u22650.\n\nStill positive. t=0.95:\n\n0.9^2=0.9025\n\nWait, t=0.95:\n\n0.95^2 \u22480.9025\n\n0.95^3\u22480.857375\n\n0.95^4\u22480.81450625\n\n0.95^5\u22480.7737809375\n\n0.95^6\u22480.7350918906\n\nSo,\n\n3*0.7350918906\u22482.205275672\n\nPlus3:\u22485.205275672\n\nMinus2*0.857375\u22481.71475:\u22485.205275672 -1.71475\u22483.490525672\n\nMinus0.7737809375\u22483.490525672 -0.7737809375\u22482.716744734\n\nMinus0.95\u22482.716744734 -0.95\u22481.766744734\n\nMinus0.81450625\u22481.766744734 -0.81450625\u22480.952238484\n\nMinus0.9025\u22480.952238484 -0.9025\u22480.049738484 \u22650.\n\nStill positive. t approaching 1 from below, P(t) approaching zero. Similarly, t approaching 1 from above:\n\nTake t=1.1:\n\n0.9 becomes 1.1:\n\n1.1^2=1.21\n\n1.1^3=1.331\n\n1.1^4=1.4641\n\n1.1^5=1.61051\n\n1.1^6=1.771561\n\nSo,\n\n3*1.771561\u22485.314683\n\nPlus3\u22488.314683\n\nMinus2*1.331\u22482.662\u22488.314683 -2.662\u22485.652683\n\nMinus1.61051\u22485.652683 -1.61051\u22484.042173\n\nMinus1.1\u22484.042173 -1.1\u22482.942173\n\nMinus1.4641\u22482.942173 -1.4641\u22481.478073\n\nMinus1.21\u22481.478073 -1.21\u22480.268073 \u22650.\n\nSo P(t) is positive here too. \n\nSince P(t) is positive at t=1, and in the regions around t=1, except at t=1 where it is zero, maybe t=1 is the only real root, and P(t) is always non-negative. If that's the case, then S is non-negative, hence the original inequality holds.\n\nBut how to show that P(t) \u22650 for all real t? Maybe factor it.\n\nLooking at P(t) = 3 t^6 - t^5 - t^4 -2 t^3 - t^2 - t +3.\n\nWait, let me write it in order:\n\nP(t) = 3 t^6 - t^5 - t^4 -2 t^3 - t^2 - t +3.\n\nHmm. Let's attempt to factor it. Since t=1 is a root, let's perform polynomial division.\n\nDivide P(t) by (t -1):\n\nUsing synthetic division:\n\nCoefficients of P(t): 3, -1, -1, -2, -1, -1, 3\n\nWait, degree 6 polynomial: leading term 3 t^6, then -t^5, -t^4, -2 t^3, -t^2, -t, +3.\n\nDividing by (t -1). Let me set up synthetic division with root t=1:\n\nBring down the 3.\n\nMultiply by 1: 3. Add to next coefficient: -1 +3=2.\n\nMultiply by1:2. Add to next coefficient: -1 +2=1.\n\nMultiply by1:1. Add to next coefficient: -2 +1=-1.\n\nMultiply by1:-1. Add to next coefficient: -1 +(-1)=-2.\n\nMultiply by1:-2. Add to next coefficient: -1 +(-2)=-3.\n\nMultiply by1:-3. Add to last term: 3 +(-3)=0. So remainder is zero, as expected.\n\nSo the polynomial factors as (t -1)(3 t^5 +2 t^4 + t^3 - t^2 -2 t -3).\n\nNow, we need to factor the quintic polynomial Q(t) = 3 t^5 +2 t^4 + t^3 - t^2 -2 t -3.\n\nQuintics are difficult, but maybe t=1 is also a root here. Let's check Q(1):\n\n3 +2 +1 -1 -2 -3 = (3+2+1) - (1+2+3) =6 -6=0. So t=1 is again a root.\n\nDivide Q(t) by (t -1):\n\nUsing synthetic division on Q(t) coefficients: 3,2,1,-1,-2,-3.\n\nBring down 3.\n\nMultiply by1:3. Add to next coefficient:2+3=5.\n\nMultiply by1:5. Add to next:1+5=6.\n\nMultiply by1:6. Add to next: -1 +6=5.\n\nMultiply by1:5. Add to next: -2 +5=3.\n\nMultiply by1:3. Add to last: -3 +3=0.\n\nSo Q(t) factors as (t -1)(3 t^4 +5 t^3 +6 t^2 +5 t +3).\n\nThus, P(t) = (t -1)^2 (3 t^4 +5 t^3 +6 t^2 +5 t +3).\n\nNow, need to check if 3 t^4 +5 t^3 +6 t^2 +5 t +3 is always positive.\n\nLet me denote R(t) = 3 t^4 +5 t^3 +6 t^2 +5 t +3.\n\nCheck if R(t) can be factored or is always positive. Let's check for real roots. Suppose R(t) =0.\n\nBut since all coefficients are positive except maybe for t negative. Let's check for t <0.\n\nLet me substitute t = -s, s >0.\n\nR(-s) = 3 s^4 -5 s^3 +6 s^2 -5 s +3.\n\nCheck if this can be zero for some s>0.\n\nLet me write R(-s) = 3 s^4 -5 s^3 +6 s^2 -5 s +3.\n\nGroup terms:\n\n= (3 s^4 +6 s^2 +3) +