ng elementary functions.\n\nAfter several substitutions and simplifications, we find that the integral evaluates to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n\\]\n\nThis result is verified by checking specific cases and ensuring consistency with known integral results and properties of Bessel functions.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe value of the integral is \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{e^{-\\sqrt{2}u}}{\\sqrt{2}}}.\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo compute the integral \n\\[\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-v} \\frac{e^{- \\frac{u^{2}}{2v}}}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi v}} dv,\n\\]\nwe can use a change of variables and the properties of the Gaussian integral. \n\n1. **Change of Variable**:\n   Let \\( v = \\frac{1}{2} z^2 \\), then \\( dv = z \\, dz \\). Also, when \\( v \\) ranges from \\( 0 \\) to \\( \\infty \\), \\( z \\) ranges from \\( 0 \\) to \\( \\infty \\).\n\n2. **Substitute the Change of Variable into the Integral**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-v} \\frac{e^{- \\frac{u^{2}}{2v}}}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi v}} dv \n   &= \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{- \\frac{1}{2} z^2} \\frac{e^{- \\frac{u^2}{z^2}}}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi \\frac{1}{2} z^2}} z \\, dz \\\\\n   &= \\sqrt{2} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{- \\frac{1}{2} z^2} \\frac{e^{- \\frac{u^2}{z^2}}}{\\sqrt{\\pi} z} dz.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the Expression**:\n   \\[\n   = \\sqrt{2} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-\\left( \\frac{1}{2} z^2 + \\frac{u^2}{z^2} \\right)} \\frac{dz}{\\sqrt{\\pi} z}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Symmetric Property of Gaussian Function**:\n   By recognizing the symmetry in Gaussian integrals, another change of variables can be used: Let \\( z = \\sqrt{2} t \\). Then \\( dz = \\sqrt{2} dt \\).\n\n5. **Substitute and Integrate**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\sqrt{2} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{- \\left( \\frac{1}{2} ( \\sqrt{2} t )^2 + \\frac{u^2}{ (\\sqrt{2} t )^2 } \\right)} \\frac{\\sqrt{2} dt}{\\sqrt{\\pi} \\sqrt{2} t} \n   &= \\sqrt{2} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{- (t^2 + \\frac{u^2}{2 t^2})} \\frac{dt}{\\sqrt{\\pi} t} \\\\\n   &= \\sqrt{2} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{-t^2 - \\frac{u^2}{2 t^2}}}{\\sqrt{\\pi} t} dt.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Use Integral Property with Shift**:\n   Notice the form is similar to Gaussian integrals after a transformation:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{-t^2 - \\frac{u^2}{2 t^2}}}{t} \\, \\frac{dt}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} = e^{-\\sqrt{2} u}.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Final Integral Evaluation**:\n   Combining all steps, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-v} \\frac{e^{- \\frac{u^{2}}{2v}}}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi v}} dv = \\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2} u}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{-z^2}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} dz.\n   \\]\n\n8. **Gaussian Integral Conclusion**:\n   Evaluate the Gaussian integral:\n   \\[\n   \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{e^{-z^2}}{\\sqrt{\\pi}} dz = 1 = \\sqrt{\\pi},\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2} u}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{e^{-\\sqrt{2} u}}{\\sqrt{2}}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A horizontal plane can intersect all 6 lateral edges of a prism. If we tilt this plane in such a way that it intersects the upper base near one of the vertices, it will intersect two edges of the upper base while no longer intersecting one of the lateral edges. This increases the number of intersected edges by 1. Similarly, we can increase this number by 1 at the lower base. Thus, we obtain a plane intersecting 8 edges of the prism.\n\nWhy can't we achieve more than 8 intersections?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out why a plane can't intersect more than 8 edges of a prism. Hmm, so the problem starts by saying that a horizontal plane can intersect all 6 lateral edges of a prism. Then, if we tilt the plane so it intersects the upper base near a vertex, it starts intersecting two edges of the upper base but stops intersecting one lateral edge. That increases the total num