e and two base edges meeting. If the plane passes through the vicinity of the vertex, it might intersect the two base edges and the lateral edge. But the problem statement says that tilting the plane so that it intersects the upper base near a vertex causes it to intersect two edges of the upper base while no longer intersecting one lateral edge. So, maybe when you tilt the plane upwards, the plane intersects two upper base edges but misses the lateral edge that was previously intersected. How does that happen?\n\nImagine the horizontal plane cutting all six lateral edges. If you tilt the plane upwards near vertex A1, the plane now slices through the upper base near A1, cutting edges A1A2 and A6A1. But because the plane is tilted, it might exit the prism before reaching the lower base, thereby no longer intersecting the lateral edge that connects to A1 (which is A1B1). Wait, but if the plane is tilted upwards near A1, wouldn't it intersect the lateral edge A1B1 higher up, near A1? Maybe not. If the plane is tilted such that it intersects the upper base edges near A1, then the part of the plane below the upper base would still intersect some lateral edges, but maybe the section near A1 causes the plane to exit the prism above some lateral edges.\n\nThis is getting a bit confusing. Maybe a diagram would help, but since I can't draw, I'll try to reason algebraically.\n\nConsider the prism as two hexagons in parallel planes, connected by vertical edges. Let\u2019s assign coordinates. Let the lower base be on the plane z = 0 and the upper base on z = 1. The vertices of the lower base are B1(1, 0, 0), B2(0.5, \u221a3/2, 0), B3(-0.5, \u221a3/2, 0), B4(-1, 0, 0), B5(-0.5, -\u221a3/2, 0), B6(0.5, -\u221a3/2, 0). The upper base vertices are A1(1, 0, 1), A2(0.5, \u221a3/2, 1), etc.\n\nA horizontal plane z = 0.5 intersects all lateral edges at their midpoints.\n\nNow, tilt the plane so that it intersects the upper base near A1. Let\u2019s say the plane passes through points nea