ects that lateral edge along its length, hence reducing the count by one, but adds two base edge intersections. Therefore, net gain of one.\n\nSimilarly, tilting at the lower base would remove another lateral edge intersection but add two lower base edges.\n\nSo, with this in mind, the maximum number of intersections would be 6 (original) - 2 (lost laterals) + 4 (gained base edges) = 8.\n\nSince each base can only contribute two edges, and each such contribution costs a lateral edge, you can't gain more than two edges per base, hence maximum of 8.\n\nAlternatively, what if you tilt the plane in such a way that it intersects three edges on one base and one on the other? But as established, due to convexity, you can't intersect three edges on a single base. Therefore, each base can only give two edges.\n\nTherefore, the total maximum is 8. Any other configuration would either fail to intersect more base edges or lose more lateral edges than gained.\n\nAnother way to think about it is that each intersection with a base edge \"replaces\" a lateral edge intersection. Since you can replace two lateral edges (one from upper tilt, one from lower tilt) with two base edge intersections each, totaling four base edges and four remaining lateral edges.\n\nHence, 8 is the maximum, and you can't go beyond that.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine why a plane cannot intersect more than 8 edges of a prism, we analyze the structure and constraints of a hexagonal prism (with 6 lateral edges, 6 upper base edges, and 6 lower base edges):\n\n1. **Horizontal Plane Intersection**:  \n   A horizontal plane parallel to the bases intersects all 6 lateral edges but none of the base edges.\n\n2. **Tilting the Plane at One Base**:  \n   When tilted near an upper base vertex, the plane intersects **2 upper base edges** (due to the convex hexagon\u2019s property that a line can cross at most 2 edges). However, this tilt causes the plane to exit the prism earlier, **missing 1 lateral edge**. Total intersections: \\(6 - 1 + 2 = 7\\).\n\n3. **Tilting at the Other Base**:  \n   Similarly, tilting near a lower base vertex intersects **2 lower base edges** but sacrifices **1 more lateral edge**. Total intersections: \\(7 - 1 + 2 = 8\\).\n\n4. **Geometric Constraints**:  \n   - A convex polygon (like the hexagonal base) allows a plane to intersect at most **2 edges per base**. Thus, the maximum contributions from the bases are \\(2 + 2 = 4\\).  \n   - Each tilt sacrifices 1 lateral edge, reducing the remaining lateral intersections to \\(6 - 2 = 4\\).  \n\n5. **Impossibility of Further Gains**:  \n   Attempting to intersect more than 2 edges on a single base violates convexity. Additional tilts would only sacrifice more lateral edges without increasing base contributions, leading to fewer total intersections.\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nThe maximum number of edges a plane can intersect in a prism is **8**, constrained by the convexity of the bases and the trade-off between lateral and base edge intersections.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Horizontal Plane Intersection (Initial State)**:\n   Let's begin by considering a scenario where a horizontal plane intersects all six lateral edges of a prism.\n\n   - Initially, this plane can be positioned to intersect the lateral edges, forming an intermediate horizontal slicing through the prism.\n\n2. **Tilting the Plane**:\n   Now, we tilt this plane in such a way that the plane intersects the top base of the prism near one of its vertices.\n\n   - By adjusting the tilt, it\u2019s evident that the plane will start intersecting two edges of the top base but will lose intersection with one of the lateral edges.\n\n3. **Change in the Number of Intersected Edges**:\n   As a result of the tilting adjustment, the plane now intersects the same number of edges from the initial state, but the specific edges have changed:\n   \n   - Initially intersecting: all 6 lateral edges.\n   - After tilting: intersects 4 lateral edges and 2 additional edges from the top base.\n   \n   \\[\\text{Total intersections} = 4 \\text{ (lateral)} + 2 \\text{ (top base)} = 6 \\]\n\n4. **Increasing the Number of Intersected Edges**:\n   Next, to further explore improving the number of intersected edges, let's tilt the plane once again but this time to intersect the bottom base near one of its vertices.\n\n   - This adjustment causes the plane to intersect two edges of the bottom base but loses intersection with another different lateral edge.\n\n5. **Resultant Intersections**:\n   Post tilting, the combination of intersected edges looks like:\n\n   - Intersects 4 remaining lateral edges.\n   - Intersects 2 edges of the top base.\n   - Intersects 2 edges of the bottom base.\n\n   \\[\\text{Total intersections} = 4 \\text{ (lateral)} + 2 \\text{ (top base)} + 2 \\text{ (bottom base)} = 8 \\]\n\n6. **Maximum Intersections Analysis**:\n   Finally, we analyze whether it\u2019s possible to get more than 8 intersected edges.\n\n   - No intersecting plane can intersect more than two edges of one base, as any further intersection would imply that the section coincides with the plane of that base.\n   - If a plane intersects 2 edges of the top base and 2 edges of the bottom base, it already eliminates the possibility of intersecting all lateral edges as there are overlapping constraints.\n\n   Hence, it confirms that increasing beyond 8 intersected edges is impossible.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe maximum number of prism edges a plane can intersect is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given point \\( F(1,0) \\), line \\( l: x=-1 \\), and line \\( l' \\perp l \\) at point \\( P \\). The perpendicular bisector of segment \\( PF \\) intersects \\( l' \\) at point \\( Q \\).\n1. Find the equation of the locus \\( C \\) of point \\( Q \\);\n2. Given point \\( H(1,2) \\), a line passing through \\( F \\) and not perpendicular to the \\( x \\)-axis inter