 adding the two inequalities is also okay since inequality directions are preserved. Then substituting the given constraints a\u2075 + b\u2075 \u22641 and c\u2075 + d\u2075 \u22641. That substitution is valid because 2/5 and 3/5 are positive coefficients, so multiplying them by the sums which are each at most 1 gives each term at most 2/5 and 3/5 respectively. Then summing those gives 1. \n\nTherefore, this approach seems valid. Hmm, this was straightforward once I thought of using weighted AM-GM. But let me see if there's another way, perhaps using H\u00f6lder's inequality, just to verify.\n\nH\u00f6lder's inequality states that for sequences (x1, x2, ..., xn) and (y1, y2, ..., yn), we have sum_{i=1}^n x_i y_i \u2264 (sum x_i^p)^{1/p} (sum y_i^q)^{1/q} where 1/p + 1/q = 1. Alternatively, for more sequences. \n\nAlternatively, H\u00f6lder's inequality can be applied with exponents that sum to 1 when considering multiple products. For example, if I have three sequences and exponents p, q, r such that 1/p + 1/q + 1/r = 1, but I'm not sure. Let's see.\n\nBut maybe in this case, since we have two variables a and c multiplied together, and similarly b and d. Let me try applying H\u00f6lder's inequality to the sum a\u00b2c\u00b3 + b\u00b2d\u00b3. Let me consider this as the sum over i=1 to 2 of (a\u00b2c\u00b3, b\u00b2d\u00b3). Wait, maybe not. Alternatively, think of the two terms as separate.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps consider that a\u00b2c\u00b3 is a product of a\u00b2 and c\u00b3, so maybe using H\u00f6lder with exponents that correspond to the reciprocal of the exponents in the terms. Let's try:\n\nLet me set up H\u00f6lder's inequality for two functions. Let me think of a\u00b2c\u00b3 as (a^2)(c^3). Suppose we have two exponents p and q such that 1/p + 1/q = 1. Then, H\u00f6lder's inequality would give that a\u00b2c\u00b3 \u2264 (a^{2p})^{1/p} (c^{3q})^{1/q} = a\u00b2 * c^3. Wait, that's trivial. Not helpful.\n\nWait, perhaps we need to use H\u00f6lder's inequality 