a + b = 0 \\implies (11 - x) + (13 - x) = 0 \\implies 24 - 2x = 0 \\implies x = 12\n   \\]\n\n9. Therefore, the roots \\(x_1, x_2, x_3\\) are 11, 12, and 13 respectively.\n\n10. The sum of the roots is:\n    \\[\n    x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 11 + 12 + 13 = 36\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{36}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A tetrahedron \\(ABCD\\) is divided into two parts by a plane parallel to \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\). It is known that the ratio of the distance from \\(AB\\) to this plane to the distance from \\(CD\\) to the plane is \\(k\\). Find the ratio of the volumes of the two parts of the tetrahedron.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. I need to find the ratio of the volumes of the two parts created by a plane that's parallel to edges AB and CD in a tetrahedron ABCD. The given ratio is k, which is the distance from AB to the plane divided by the distance from CD to the plane. Hmm. Let me start by visualizing the tetrahedron.\n\nFirst, a tetrahedron has four triangular faces. AB and CD are two edges that are skew, I think. Since the plane is parallel to both AB and CD, it must intersect the other edges of the tetrahedron. Wait, which edges are those? The tetrahedron has edges AB, AC, AD, BC, BD, and CD. If the plane is parallel to AB and CD, it can't intersect AB or CD because it's parallel to them. So it must intersect the other edges: AC, BD, BC, and AD? Hmm, maybe not all of them. Let me think.\n\nActually, in a tetrahedron, if you have a plane parallel to two skew edges, the plane will intersect the other edges. Since AB and CD are skew (they don't intersect and aren't parallel), the plane parallel to both must intersect the remaining edges. Let's try to figure out how.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's easier to consider coordinates. Let me assign coordinates to the vertices to make this concrete. Let's place point A at (0, 0, 0) and point B at (1, 0, 0). Since AB is along t