ints \\(A\\), \\(D\\), and \\(G\\) lie on a straight line in that order.\n   - Line \\(\\overline{CF}\\) intersects \\(\\overline{AG}\\) at point \\(X\\).\n\n2. **Identifying Lengths and Positions:**\n   - Since \\(DEFG\\) is a square with side length \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\), and \\(E\\) is on \\(\\overline{CD}\\), we determine that \\(E\\) divides \\(CD\\) into parts of lengths \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) (for \\(CE\\)) and \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) (for \\(ED\\)).\n   - The length \\(AG\\) is represented by adding up the segments that create squares as we move from \\(A\\) to \\(X\\).\n\n3. **Constructing Geometric Series:**\n   - Notice that each additional square formed between \\(\\overline{AX}\\) and \\(\\overline{XC}\\) is successively scaled down by a factor of \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\).\n   - The side length of the nth square in this chain of inscribed squares can be described by a geometric series where each term is \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of the previous term.\n\n4. **Express the Length \\(AX\\) as a Geometric Series:**\n   - The length \\(AX\\) can be expressed in terms of an infinite geometric sum:\n     \\[\n     AX = 1 + \\frac{1}{3} + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3 + \\cdots\n     \\]\n   - This is an infinite geometric series with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\n5. **Sum of the Infinite Geometric Series:**\n   - The sum \\(S\\) of an infinite geometric series is given by the formula \\(S = \\frac{a}{1 - r}\\):\n     \\[\n     AX = \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Simplifying and Finding \\(100m + n\\):**\n   - From the series sum, \\(AX = \\frac{3}{2}\\), we identify \\(m = 3\\) and \\(n = 2\\).\n   - Therefore, we calculate \\(100m + n\\) as follows:\n     \\[\n     100m + n = 100 \\times 3 + 2 = 302\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\(\\boxed{302}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\(0<\\