es \\left( \\frac{|a - b|}{2} \\right) = 25 \\).\n\nSince area is always positive, maybe we can drop the absolute value and assume \\( a > b \\) without loss of generality. So:\n\n\\( \\left( \\frac{a + b}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{a - b}{2} \\right) = 25 \\).\n\nMultiplying these together:\n\n\\( \\frac{(a + b)(a - b)}{4} = 25 \\).\n\nSimplify the numerator:\n\n\\( \\frac{a^2 - b^2}{4} = 25 \\).\n\nMultiply both sides by 4:\n\n\\( a^2 - b^2 = 100 \\).\n\nAlright, that's equation one.\n\nNow, the problem also states that the height \\( h \\) of the trapezoid is three times the difference of its bases. So:\n\n\\( h = 3(a - b) \\).\n\nWe need to find the area of the trapezoid. The area \\( S \\) of a trapezoid is given by:\n\n\\( S = \\frac{(a + b)}{2} \\times h \\).\n\nWe already know \\( \\frac{a + b}{2} \\) is the midline \\( m \\), so \\( S = m \\times h \\).\n\nBut let's express everything in terms of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n\nGiven \\( h = 3(a - b) \\), substitute into the area formula:\n\n\\( S = \\frac{a + b}{2} \\times 3(a - b) = \\frac{3(a + b)(a - b)}{2} \\).\n\nFrom equation one, we have \\( a^2 - b^2 = 100 \\), which is \\( (a + b)(a - b) = 100 \\).\n\nTherefore, substitute that into the area expression:\n\n\\( S = \\frac{3 \\times 100}{2} = \\frac{300}{2} = 150 \\).\n\nWait, so the area is 150? Let me check this again to make sure.\n\nFirst, midline \\( m = \\frac{a + b}{2} \\), segment \\( MN = \\frac{a - b}{2} \\), product is \\( \\frac{(a + b)(a - b)}{4} = 25 \\), so \\( (a + b)(a - b) = 100 \\).\n\nHeight \\( h = 3(a - b) \\), area is \\( \\frac{(a + b)}{2} \\times h = \\frac{(a + b)}{2} \\times 3(a - b) = \\frac{3}{2} \\times (a + b)(a - b) = \\frac{3}{2} \\times 100 = 150 \\).\n\nYes, that seems correct. Therefore, the area of the trapezoid is 150.\n\nBut let me just verify if all steps are valid. We assumed \\( a > b \\), which is fine because the difference is squared in the product. Also, all the formulas used are standard trapezoid p